Todd-Coxeter over the trivial subgroup: |G| = 120
orders: a -> 10, b -> 6, ab -> 4, z = a^5 = b^3 = (ab)^2 -> 2
centre Z(G) = {1, z}, z = a^5 of order 2
derived subgroup order 120 (perfect: True)
abelianization: relation matrix [[5,-3],[-2,1]] (exponent sums of a, b), determinant -1, so G^ab = 0
element-order census: {1: 1, 2: 1, 3: 20, 4: 30, 5: 24, 6: 20, 10: 24}
a -> (1 4 3 2 5), b -> (1 2 3): well-defined homomorphism G -> A_5, onto (60 even permutations), kernel = {1, z} = Z(G)
isomorphism G -> SL(2,5): a -> (0, 1, 4, 3), b -> (0, 4, 1, 1) (entries a11,a12,a21,a22 mod 5); bijective; z -> -I
Hence G = <a,b | a^5 = b^3 = (ab)^2> is the binary icosahedral group 2I = SL(2,5), order 120, perfect,
centre {1,z} = {+-1}, and the A_5 map of the return is the quotient by the centre, G/Z(G) = PSL(2,5) = A_5.
